# 三、§2：三个方案

三个方案都要做的两处必改：
- Requirements (1)："The foundation model must be generic" 改成 "The model must be generic"。
- Challenges (1)："The foundation model must learn task-agnostic representations" 改成 "The model must learn task-agnostic representations"。

## 方案 1：保守版（约 −0.07 页）

结构全部保留，只去掉 foundation model 的说法、改语法、删一两处冗余。

**§2.1 第一段：**
The Radio Access Network (RAN), a core component of cellular infrastructure, consists of widely deployed base stations that provide radio coverage across geographic regions. An LTE base station (eNodeB) typically comprises multiple directional sectors (commonly three), each hosting one or more cells, which are the management units for scheduling, configuration, and performance measurement. Performance metrics may also be aggregated at the sector or base-station level. Accurate and efficient RAN profiling, used to predict behaviors such as traffic growth, congestion risk, and performance degradation, is essential for numerous engineering tasks, including configuration management and tuning [8, 9], troubleshooting [30, 31], and mobility management [11].

**§2.1 第二段：**
The states and behavior of RANs are captured by heterogeneous data, such as RAN property data, runtime performance measurements, log data, and geographic information, among which the first two are the most widely used. (1) RAN property data include configuration parameters that describe cell hardware, software setup, and operational settings, along with long-term performance indicators that capture stable behaviors over time. Because these attributes are structured fields (numerical or categorical) whose order carries no semantic meaning, the data are naturally represented in tabular form. (2) Runtime performance data consist of time-varying metrics collected at different spatial granularities (cell or sector) and temporal intervals (e.g., hourly). They reflect dynamic network conditions and exhibit temporal dependencies, making them naturally suited for time-series representation.

**§2.2 第一、二段：** 不动。

**§2.2 第三段：**
Inspired by large pretrained models (e.g., [2, 43]), which are trained once on massive historical datasets and then efficiently adapted to diverse downstream tasks [3, 16, 19, 21], we aim to develop a multimodal pretrained model for RAN profiling. Our goal is to jointly learn from RAN property data and runtime performance measurements to characterize the operational behaviors of cellular network cells and sectors. By learning generic representations across heterogeneous RAN conditions, the model enables lightweight fine-tuning and efficient adaptation to a wide range of downstream network management tasks.

**Requirements、Challenges：** 只做上面的两处必改。

## 方案 2：中间版（约 −0.15 页）

在保守版基础上，只多压一处：§2.2 第二段（规则方法、任务专用模型）。这段和 Intro 第三段讲的是同一件事，四句压成一句，引用全部保留。

**§2.2 第二段替换成：**
Legacy rule-based RAN control systems struggle with the high dimensionality and heterogeneity of modern RAN data, leading to incomplete coverage and corner-case failures, while learning-based approaches [15, 26, 30, 31, 34, 42] require supervised training tailored to a specific use case and dataset, so operators must repeatedly redesign feature pipelines and retrain models for new applications.

其余和保守版一样。

## 方案 3：精简版（约 −0.5 页）

§2.1 合成一段，§2.2 前三段合成一段。和 Intro 重复的内容全部删掉，Requirements 和 Challenges 保留。

**§2.1（替换两段）：**
The RAN consists of base stations that provide radio coverage across geographic regions. An LTE base station (eNodeB) typically comprises three directional sectors, each hosting one or more cells, which are the units of scheduling, configuration, and performance measurement; metrics may also be aggregated at the sector or base-station level. RAN profiling, i.e., predicting behaviors such as traffic growth, congestion risk, and performance degradation, underlies many engineering tasks, including configuration tuning [8, 9], troubleshooting [30, 31], and mobility management [11]. Two data types are the most widely used: RAN property data, comprising configuration parameters and long-term performance indicators stored as structured, order-agnostic tabular fields, and runtime performance data, comprising time-varying KPIs collected at cell or sector granularity and at hourly intervals, which exhibit temporal structure.

**§2.2（替换前三段）：**
RAN operators rely on the profile of each sector, i.e., its configuration properties and measurement data, to make operational decisions and detect anomalies, increasingly through automated control loops. As discussed in Section 1, rule-based systems struggle with the dimensionality and heterogeneity of modern RAN data, and learning-based models [15, 26, 30, 31, 34, 42] are trained for a single task and dataset, so operators must repeatedly redesign feature pipelines and retrain models. Our goal is therefore to jointly learn from RAN property data and runtime measurements representations of sectors that are reusable across downstream network management tasks and can be adapted through lightweight fine-tuning.

